(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-pulmonary-pathophysiology-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pulmonary Pathophysiology Quiz 2 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which mast-cell mediator in allergic asthma is the slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis?","choices":["Histamine","Serotonin","Bradykinin","Leukotrienes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mast cells release histamine, slow-reacting substances of anaphylaxis (leukotrienes), prostaglandins, bradykinins, eosinophilic chemotactic factor and serotonin. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What does gross aspiration deliver to the lower respiratory tract?","choices":["Large numbers of anaerobic bacteria","Inorganic dust particles","Inhaled organic antigens","Blood-borne staphylococci"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gross aspiration can deliver large numbers of anaerobic bacteria to the lower respiratory tract, a major contributor to anaerobic lung infection and abscess formation. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"Which mechanism produces a transudative pleural effusion?","choices":["Increased permeability of inflamed pleura","Impaired lymphatic drainage","Leakage of chyle from the thoracic duct","Raised hydrostatic or lowered oncotic pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Transudates are caused by increased hydrostatic or decreased oncotic pressure, as in severe heart failure, cirrhosis with ascites, nephrotic syndrome and myxedema. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 93, 94, 95"},{"q":"What forms the hyaline membranes of acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Collagen from proliferating fibroblasts","Calcified immune complexes","Inflammatory deposition of proteins","Inhaled inorganic dust"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In acute respiratory distress syndrome, hyaline membranes are associated with inflammatory deposition of proteins. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which lung carcinoma grows fastest, doubling in about 33 days?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Small cell (oat cell) carcinoma grows rapidly, doubling in about 33 days, with common widespread metastasis and paraneoplastic syndrome. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 25, 26, 27"},{"q":"Which finding fits non-allergic (intrinsic) asthma?","choices":["An IgE-mediated immediate phase","A more favorable prognosis","Negative skin tests, normal serum IgE","A specific allergen identified"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intrinsic asthma has no identifiable specific allergen, negative skin tests to common inhalant allergens and normal serum IgE; it often develops in middle age. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which description fits the normal pulmonary circulation?","choices":["Low flow, high pressure","High flow, high pressure","High flow, low pressure","Low flow, low pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pulmonary circulation is normally a high-flow, low-pressure system: about 22/8 to 25/8 mmHg, mean 15 mmHg. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How do fever, infection and burns contribute to hypercapnic ventilatory failure?","choices":["They increase dead space","They block sensing of carbon dioxide","They raise carbon dioxide production","They cut the respiratory rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypercapnic failure is alveolar ventilation inadequate for the carbon dioxide produced, and fever, infection and trauma or burns increase that production. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which of the three alveolar patterns of diffuse interstitial lung disease is reversible?","choices":["Inflammation","Fibrosis","Destruction","Bullae formation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The immune response shows three patterns: inflammation (early and reversible), fibrosis, and destruction (end stage). — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 72, 73, 74"},{"q":"How does mitral stenosis lead to pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["It increases pulmonary blood flow","It causes hypoxic vasoconstriction","It raises blood viscosity","It raises left atrial pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis, mitral regurgitation and left ventricular failure cause pulmonary hypertension by increasing left atrial pressure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which cough pattern defines chronic bronchitis?","choices":["Productive cough over 3 months a year for 2 years","Productive cough over 3 weeks after a viral illness","Dry nighttime cough for 2 months","Purulent sputum from infected bronchial pockets"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic bronchitis is hypersecretion of bronchial mucus with a chronic or recurrent productive cough of more than 3 months each year for 2 or more successive years. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Antibodies formed against inhaled organic particles in hypersensitivity pneumonitis produce which hypersensitivity type?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibodies produced to inhaled organic particles cause a type III reaction: antigen-antibody complexes deposit in the lung, initiating inflammation and granulomas. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Why is the pulmonary edema of acute respiratory distress syndrome called noncardiogenic?","choices":["It comes from left atrial pressure","It comes from low plasma oncotic pressure","It is a low-protein transudate","It comes from leaky capillaries"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Injury to the alveolar-capillary membrane increases vascular permeability, flooding the alveoli with protein-rich fluid: noncardiogenic pulmonary edema from leaky capillaries. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 84, 85"},{"q":"How does nephrotic syndrome cause a pleural effusion?","choices":["It raises vascular hydrostatic pressure","It lowers plasma oncotic pressure","It lowers pleural space pressure","It inflames the pleural membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Decreased plasma oncotic pressure, from severe hypoalbuminemia, nephrotic syndrome (protein loss) or cirrhosis (less albumin synthesis), lets fluid collect in the pleural space. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 93, 94"},{"q":"What feature defines the obstructive pulmonary diseases?","choices":["Decreased expansion of the lungs","Increased airflow resistance","Thickening of the alveolar interstitium","Air in the pleural space"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obstructive pulmonary diseases are manifested by increased airflow resistance, whether from the airway wall, loss of parenchyma or material in the lumen. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Approximately what is the normal mean pulmonary arterial pressure in a healthy adult?","choices":["8 mmHg","15 mmHg","30 mmHg","45 mmHg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal pulmonary arterial pressure is about 22/8 to 25/8 mmHg, with a mean of 15 mmHg. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What links each obstructive event to cardiac and cerebrovascular disturbance?","choices":["Immune complexes in vessel walls","Emboli from the pharynx","Steady hyperventilation through the night","Asphyxia with sudden autonomic arousal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With each obstruction, progressive asphyxia (lack of oxygen, excess carbon dioxide) and sudden autonomic and behavioral arousal result in acute cardiac and cerebrovascular disturbances. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 102, 103"},{"q":"Which bronchiectasis shape combines saccular and cylindrical features?","choices":["Saccular","Cylindrical","Fusiform","Centriacinar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bronchiectasis is classified by bronchial shape: saccular (cavity-like), cylindrical, and fusiform (varicose), which combines saccular and cylindrical. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 62, 63"},{"q":"What defines emphysema?","choices":["Alveolar wall thickening with fibrosis","Reversible airway inflammation","Permanent dilation of the bronchi","Alveolar wall destruction without fibrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema (type A chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, the “pink puffer”) is destruction of alveolar walls without fibrosis and abnormal enlargement of distal air sacs. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which finding confirms a restrictive pattern?","choices":["Low forced vital capacity alone","Low total lung capacity","Low FEV1/FVC ratio","High residual volume"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A low forced vital capacity alone does not establish restriction; a low total lung capacity confirms the restrictive pattern. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 59, 82"},{"q":"For a patient already hospitalized for another reason, within what time of admission must respiratory symptoms begin for the pneumonia to count as community-acquired?","choices":["Under 12 hours","Under 24 hours","Under 48 hours","Under 96 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients hospitalized for other reasons for under 48 hours before respiratory symptoms develop are also called community-acquired. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 108, 109"},{"q":"Why does tension pneumothorax reduce cardiac output?","choices":["Air enters the coronary arteries","The shifted mediastinum cuts venous return","The lung re-expands too quickly","Fluid tamponades the heart"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Air enters but cannot escape on expiration and builds up under pressure; the ipsilateral lung collapses and the mediastinum is forced to the contralateral side, decreasing venous return and cardiac output. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 90, 91"},{"q":"Which category of acute respiratory failure shows hypoxemia with a normal or low carbon dioxide?","choices":["Hypoxic respiratory failure","Hypercarbic ventilatory failure","Combined respiratory and ventilatory failure","Acute respiratory distress syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Failure of respiration or oxygenation gives hypoxemia with normal or lower carbon dioxide: hypoxic respiratory failure. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is a mechanism of HYPOXIC respiratory failure?","choices":["Shunt","Increased carbon dioxide production","Inability to sense raised carbon dioxide","Failure to signal effector muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxic respiratory failure arises from ventilation/perfusion mismatch, shunt, decreased diffusion, alveolar hypoventilation and high altitude. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 9, 10"},{"q":"What characterizes the end-stage (destruction) pattern of diffuse interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Hyperinflated lungs with air trapped in the sacs","Dilated bronchi that act as pockets of infection","Lost alveolar walls and stiff, noncompliant lungs","Reversible inflammation filling the alveoli"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The destruction stage is end-stage disease: alveolar walls are lost and the scarred lungs become stiff and noncompliant. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What mediates the immediate phase of allergic (extrinsic) asthma?","choices":["IgE on mast cells","Sensitized T cells","Complement membrane pores","Neutrophil proteases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The immediate phase is mediated by IgE to specific antigens; IgE binds mast cells, which release chemical mediators when the antigen arrives. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What happens to the airways in acute bronchitis?","choices":["Destruction of alveolar walls without fibrosis","Permanent dilation of the bronchi","Gland hypertrophy over successive years","Inflammation, more mucus, lost ciliary function"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In acute bronchitis the airways are inflamed, narrow and swollen, with increased mucus, loss of ciliary function and loss of portions of the ciliated epithelium. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"What does alpha-1 antitrypsin normally do in the lung?","choices":["Inhibits breakdown of alveolar tissue","Secretes surfactant into the alveoli","Releases proteases from neutrophils","Clears mucus from the bronchi"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alpha-1 antitrypsin is a protective enzyme that inhibits proteolytic breakdown of alveolar tissue; its hereditary deficiency causes about 1% of emphysema. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 53, 54"},{"q":"What is the basic lesion of bronchiectasis?","choices":["Alveolar destruction without fibrosis","Wall destruction that dilates the bronchi","Reversible smooth muscle spasm","Gland hypertrophy narrowing the lumen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In bronchiectasis destruction of the bronchial walls results in dilation of the bronchi: an obstructive, suppurative disorder. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which disorder obstructs airflow through material within the airway lumen?","choices":["Emphysema","Cystic fibrosis","Asthma","Chronic bronchitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obstruction of the airway lumen includes bronchiectasis, bronchiolitis, cystic fibrosis, acute tracheobronchial obstruction, epiglottitis, croup and foreign body. — 9. Pulmonary Pathophysiology - Ward 2026 (rebuilt from recording).pptx, Slides 33, 34"}]};
